index 0000000..d4a959b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/FIXES-NOT-APPLIED.md
@@ -0,0 +1,217 @@
+# Fixes deliberately NOT applied
+
+39 fixes are in the patch. These are the ones I left alone, and why. All of them
+would change observable behaviour in a way I could not verify from the source
+alone — so applying them silently would be worse than leaving them documented.
+
+Each entry says what I would need to decide it.
+
+---
+
+## 1. `GfxBlendMode` / `GfxAlphaMode` ordinals vs their own documentation
+
+**File:** `Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx/Gfx.cs`
+
+Each enum sits directly beneath a comment block giving the GL bit code for every
+value. The declared orders disagree with those comments — `GfxBlendMode` on
+**all 11 values**, `GfxAlphaMode` on 5 of 8:
+
+| ordinal | `GfxAlphaMode` declares | comment says |
+|---|---|---|
+| `010` | `LEqual` | `GL_EQUAL` |
+| `011` | `Equal` | `GL_LEQUAL` |
+| `100` | `GEqual` | `GL_GREATER` |
+| `101` | `Greater` | `GL_NOTEQUAL` |
+| `110` | `NotEqual` | `GL_GEQUAL` |
+
+`GfxBlendMode` diverges from ordinal 0 onward (`Zero` where the comment says
+`GL_ONE`).
+
+**Why not fixed:** either those ordinals are read from or written to disk — in
+which case every material's blend mode is wrong — or the comments are stale and
+merely misleading. I cannot tell which, because every consumer lives in a
+platform crate that is itself unported. Reordering the enum would silently
+change every serialized material; deleting the comments would discard the only
+documentation.
+
+**To decide:** check whether any format reader or writer casts an integer to
+these enums. If yes, the comments are authoritative and the enum order is a bug.
+If no, delete the comments.
+
+The Rust port keeps the declared order and adds `from_gl_code`/`to_gl_code`
+following the documented table, so both readings are available and separable.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The three binary16 implementations disagree on rounding
+
+**Files:** `Core/OpenStack.PolyIO/System/HalfFloat.cs`,
+`Core/OpenStack.Polyfills/Poly2.1/Half.cs`,
+`Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx.Other/Algorithms/HalfPrecConverter.cs`
+
+All three convert between `float` and IEEE binary16. `HalfFloat` **truncates**
+toward zero; the other two round to **nearest-even** (which IEEE requires). So
+the same value converted through different paths can differ by one ULP
+depending on which type the caller happened to reach for.
+
+**Why not fixed:** consolidating on one is a design change across three call
+graphs, and changing `HalfFloat`'s rounding would alter the bytes written by any
+format that uses it. Which of the three should survive is a call about the
+codebase, not a bug fix.
+
+**To decide:** pick one implementation (`System.Half` on modern .NET makes all
+three redundant) and check whether any on-disk format depends on `HalfFloat`'s
+truncation.
+
+---
+
+## 3. `MurmurHash2` / `MurmurHash3` conventions
+
+**Files:** `Core/OpenStk/Algorithms/MurmurHash2.cs`, `MurmurHash3.cs`
+
+Three deviations from the references, all preserved:
+
+* `MurmurHash2` short-circuits empty input to `0`; the reference runs the final
+  mix and returns a seed-dependent value.
+* `MurmurHash2` hashes strings as **ASCII** (so non-ASCII becomes `?` and
+  collides); `MurmurHash3` hashes them as **UTF-16**. The two disagree on
+  identical string input.
+* `MurmurHash3` uses a seed of `0xFFFFFFFF`, not the conventional `0`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** any of these changes every hash the codebase produces. If
+hashes are persisted anywhere — a cache key, an asset index, a file name — a
+"fix" invalidates all of it.
+
+**To decide:** whether any hash from these is written to disk or compared across
+runs. If not, align both on UTF-8 and the reference seeds. If so, leave them and
+document that they are not reference-compatible.
+
+---
+
+## 4. `Camera.CopyFrom` does not copy `Scale`
+
+**File:** `Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx.Egin/Egin_Render.cs`
+
+It copies `Location`, `Pitch`, `Yaw`, and all three matrices — but not `Scale`.
+So a camera copied from one with a non-default scale keeps its own scale while
+inheriting matrices built with the other's, and the two disagree until the next
+`RecalculateMatrices`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** "fixing" it means choosing a semantic. Copying `Scale` makes
+the copy identical; not copying it and recalculating makes the copy consistent
+but different from the source. Both are defensible and the call sites would tell
+you which was intended.
+
+---
+
+## 5. `Decay` and `FadeAndKill` both decrement `Lifetime`
+
+**File:** `Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx.Egin/Egin_Particle.cs`
+
+A KV blob listing both operators — nothing prevents it — ages particles at twice
+the intended rate.
+
+**Why not fixed:** which operator should own the decrement is a data question.
+Removing it from either changes the behaviour of every existing particle
+definition that relies on the current arrangement.
+
+---
+
+## 6. `Particle.GetRotationMatrix` ignores `Rotation.X`
+
+**File:** `Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx.Egin/Egin_Particle.cs`
+
+It composes only `Rotation.Z` and `Rotation.Y`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** this may well be deliberate — billboarded particles commonly
+have no roll about the view axis. Adding X rotation would change the appearance
+of every particle system.
+
+---
+
+## 7. `Grammar.Pluralize`'s `us -> uss` rule
+
+**File:** `Core/OpenStack.Polyfills/System.Globalization/Grammar.cs`
+
+`Sarcophagus` pluralises to `Sarcophaguss`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** the C# carries a comment citing a packet capture — the game
+server itself emits this, so the point is matching the server's text, not
+correct Latin. Fixing it would make the client disagree with the server.
+
+---
+
+## 8. CDI's `i <= NumSessions` loop
+
+**File:** `Vfx/OpenStack.Vfx/Disc.cs`
+
+The session loop runs one iteration past the declared count and adds every pass
+to `Sessions`, so `Sessions.Count` is always `NumSessions + 1`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** the extra pass appears intentional — the empty-session guard
+(`if (NumTracks == 0 && i != NumSessions)`) exempts exactly the last one, so it
+is reading a terminator record. The bug is that the terminator is *stored as
+data*, which any caller iterating `Sessions` then sees. Fixing it means deciding
+whether to stop storing it (and possibly breaking a caller that indexes by
+session number) or to keep it and filter at the accessors.
+
+The Rust port keeps the parse and exposes `sessions()` and
+`sessions_including_terminator()` separately.
+
+---
+
+## 9. `SS_Leadout`'s lead-in offset
+
+**File:** `Vfx/OpenStack.Vfx/Disc.cs`
+
+`Timestamp` is computed relative to the lead-out track and `AP_Timestamp`
+absolute, with no `+ 150` on either — while
+`Synthesize_DiscTOCFromRawTOCEntries` subtracts 150 when reading one back. One
+of the two is off by the lead-in.
+
+**Why not fixed:** I could not determine which side is wrong without a real
+image to compare against. Guessing would move the error rather than remove it.
+
+---
+
+## 10. `TextureHelper.GetBlockSize` throws unconditionally
+
+**File:** `Gfx/OpenStack.Gfx/TextureHelper.cs`
+
+Its entire body is `throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(...)` — no switch, no
+lookup. `GetMipmapTrueDataSize` calls it on its first line, so **that function
+has never returned a value.**
+
+**Why not fixed:** filling it in is implementing a missing feature, not fixing a
+defect. The mapping is derivable from the DDS FourCC table elsewhere in the same
+project (`DXT1` → 8 bytes/block, `DXT3`/`DXT5` → 16, and so on), and the Rust
+port does exactly that in `texture_helper.rs`'s `BlockFormat` — but writing new
+behaviour into the C# under the heading of a bug fix seemed wrong without asking.
+
+**To decide:** whether you want the mapping ported back. It is about 15 lines and
+`openstack-gfx/src/texture_helper.rs` has it.
+
+---
+
+## 11. `X_LumpNO2` and `X_Lump2NO` are field-identical
+
+**File:** `Core/OpenStack.PolyIO/System.IO/Polyfill.cs`
+
+Both are `{ int num; int offset; int offset2; }`.
+
+**Why not fixed:** likely a copy-paste slip, but collapsing them changes a public
+type surface, and it is possible the distinction is intended to be meaningful to
+readers even where the layout matches.
+
+---
+
+## Also not applied: everything in `OpenStack.Phy2`
+
+`phy2` does not compile — 107 of its 164 files reference 21 namespaces that
+exist nowhere in the solution (`ACE.Entity.Enum`,
+`ACE.Server.Physics.Common`, ...) with no package or project reference supplying
+them. Its documented defects (`PhysicsGlobals.DefaultSortingSphere` is
+permanently null; `Ray(startPoint, offset)` discards its start point on the
+degenerate path and its guard tests the wrong quantity) are real, but patching a
+project that cannot build is not useful until the ACE dependency question is
+settled.
