+* `github.com/aappleby/smhasher` — the original. Public domain, author
+  disclaims copyright. Effectively unmaintained (its own wiki says the figures
+  are stale; last edited 2016), but it is the canonical algorithm.
+* `github.com/rurban/smhasher` — the maintained fork, and where the table above
+  lives.
+* `gitlab.com/fwojcik/smhasher3` — SMHasher3, a rewrite based on rurban's.
+
+### What it showed
+
+**`MurmurHash3`'s algorithm is correct** — the implementation reproduces
+`0xB0F57EE3` exactly, so the only deviation was its seed (`0xFFFFFFFF` rather
+than `0`). And `MurmurHash3` has **zero call sites** in the solution, so the
+seed choice is moot. Left alone.
+
+**`MurmurHash2`'s empty-input short circuit was a genuine deviation** and is
+now **fixed**. The verification procedure hashes a zero-length key with seed
+256, where the reference gives `0xD1E836D3` and `if (l == 0) return 0;`
+returned 0 regardless of seed. Every non-empty input already agreed exactly, so
+that guard was the whole of the difference.
+
+### Why it became safe to apply
+
+The blocker was hash stability — a fix changes every hash, which matters if any
+is persisted. There is exactly **one** call site in the solution:
+
+    Platforms/OpenStack.Platform.OpenGL/Gfx/OpenGL.cs:93
+    ShaderLoader.CalculateShaderCacheHash -> MurmurHash2.Hash(b.ToString(), ShaderSeed)
+
+Its result keys `readonly Dictionary<uint, Shader> CachedShaders` — an in-memory
+dictionary rebuilt every run. Nothing is written to disk or compared across
+processes, so no stored value could be invalidated.
+
+### Still open: the string encoding
+
+`MurmurHash2.Hash(string)` uses `Encoding.ASCII`, so any non-ASCII character
+becomes `?` and collides. `MurmurHash3` uses UTF-16 for the same job, so the two
+disagree on identical input.
+
+In practice the one call site hashes shader names and preprocessor define keys,
+which are ASCII identifiers, so this is currently harmless. UTF-8 for both would
+be the conventional choice, and is a one-line change if you want it.
+
+### On taking a NuGet dependency instead
+
+I looked; I would not. There is no clear winner, and the case for a dependency
+is weak here:
+
+| package | state |
+|---|---|
+| `MurmurHash-net-core` | Most downloaded (~1.7M), but last updated 2017, targets netstandard1.6 |
+| `System.Data.HashFunction.MurmurHash` | 2018, .NET Framework 4.5; NuGet notes it is "not used by any popular GitHub repositories" |
+| `MurmurHash.Net` | Still at 0.0.2 (2020) |
+| `CloudMesh.MurmurHash` | Modern targeting (net6-8), derived from Akka.net's code |
+| `JeremyEspresso.MurmurHash` | Modern, `Span`-based, zero-allocation |
+| `MurmurIncrementalHash` | net6.0, supports incremental hashing |
+
+Against that: this solution targets **net10.0**, the two files are **28 and 38
+live lines**, they are now verified against the authoritative reference, and
+they have **one call site between them**. Adding a single-maintainer dependency
+last touched in 2017-2020 to replace 66 verified lines is a net increase in
+risk.
+
+The better question is whether MurmurHash is needed at all here. The one use is
+an in-memory dictionary key with no stability or distribution requirement —
+`HashCode.Combine` over the name and the sorted defines, or simply keying the
+dictionary on the built string, would remove both files. If you want the
+dependency anyway, `CloudMesh.MurmurHash` or `JeremyEspresso.MurmurHash` are the
+two I would consider on target-framework grounds.
+
+Either way, add the verification value as a test — it is about 15 lines and it
+is the check that would have caught the short circuit. The Rust port has it in
+`core/openstack/src/algorithms/murmur_hash3.rs` as
+`passes_the_smhasher_verification_test`.
 
